<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.15.2: The Vertex Form</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02009</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.15.2: The Vertex Form</md:title>
    <md:uuid>b7f7d3c0-39c6-400a-ab8d-8338efcf3183</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <para>Here are two sets of equations for quadratic functions you saw earlier. In each set, the expressions that define the output are equivalent. In other words, all the equations in Set 1 define the same function. The same is true for Set 2: all the equations define the same function but they are just written in different forms.</para>
      <para>
       <emphasis>Set 1:</emphasis>
    </para>
      <para>\(f(x)=x^2+4x\)</para>
      <para>\(g(x)=x(x+4)\)</para>
      <para>\(h(x)=(x+2)^2−4\)</para>
      <para>
       <emphasis>Set 2:</emphasis>
    </para>
      <para>\(p(x)=-x^2+6x−5\)</para>
      <para>\(q(x)=(5−x)(x−1)\)</para>
      <para>\(r(x)=-1(x−3)^2+4\)</para>
      <para>The expression that defines \(h\) is written in <term class="glossary-tooltip">vertex form</term>. We can show that it is equivalent to the expression defining \(f\) by expanding and simplifying the expression:</para>
      <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}(x+2)^2-4&amp;=&amp;(x+2)(x+2)-4\\&amp;=&amp;x^2+2x+2x+4-4\\&amp;=&amp;x^2+4x,\;\mathrm{which}\;\mathrm{is}\;f(x)\end{array}\)</para>
      <para>If this simplified expression is factored, then we arrive at the expression that defines function \(g\): \(x^2+4x=x(x+4)\). The expression that defines \(g\) is written in <term class="glossary-tooltip">factored form</term>. The simplified expression that defines function \(f\) is written in <term class="glossary-tooltip">standard form</term>.</para>
      
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Show that the expressions defining \(r\) and \(p\) are equivalent.</para>
            <para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>s</emphasis><emphasis>olution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your Answer:</para>
            <para>Expanding and simplifying function \(r\), \(-1(x−3)^2+4 gives -1(x^2−6x+9)+4\), which equals \(-x^2+6x−9+4\) or \(-x^2+6x−5\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <para>For questions 2 – 3, examine the graphs representing the quadratic functions.</para>
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <title>Activity</title>
        <exercise id="ffff5156-2bf7-4567-96b2-7aa7f7191d18" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>

            <para>Examine the graph of the function \(h\).</para>
            <para>What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph of \(h\)?</para>
            
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! -2</para>
            <para>The correct answer is -2.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_467b3ca3-8550-4222-ba3f-a1c76865ea1b" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            
            <para>What is the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph of \(h\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! -4</para>
            <para>The correct answer is -4.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_0773894f-eec9-475a-82ba-23df40c73c4f" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>

            <para>Examine the graph of the function \(r\).</para>
            <para>What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph of \(r\)?</para>
            
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 3</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 3.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="abdf1679-d62b-4b66-bdb8-979b6c361039" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            
            <para>What is the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph of \(r\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 4</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 4.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Why do you think expressions such as those defining \(h\) and \(r\) are said to be written in vertex form?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>The numbers in vertex form seem to give the coordinates of the vertex of the graph. When a positive number is added to the \(x\) in the parentheses, the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex seems to be negative—which is the opposite of that number. When a number is subtracted from \(x\), the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex seems to be positive—which is the opposite of what it looks like.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <note>
        <title>Why Should I Care?</title>
          <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_17_03_01.png"/>
          </media>
        
        <para>When you launch an object, it does not travel in a straight line. Instead, it follows a curved path called a parabola. This means you have to account for distance, height, and angle if you want to hit your target. You can use quadratic equations to find these measurements.</para>
        <para>The next time you throw a ball, think about the parabolic path that it follows.</para>
      </note>
    </section>

    <note class="self-check">
      <para>Which equation is equivalent to \(y=(x-3)^2-9\)?</para>
    </note>

    <section><title>Equivalent Forms of Quadratics</title>
      <para>Quadratics can be written in different equivalent forms:</para>
      <list>
        <item>Standard form: \(y=ax^2+bx+c\)</item>
        <item>Factored form: \(y = (x + a)(x + b)\)</item>
        <item>Vertex form: \(y=a(x-h)^2+k\)</item>
      </list>
      <para>Example</para>
      <para>Write \(y=(x+2)^2-9\) in standard and factored forms.</para>
      <para>Here is how to find the equivalent standard form:</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Expand the vertex form to multiply the binomials.
        \(y=(x+2)(x+2)-9\)</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Multiply the binomials.
        \(y=x^2+2x+2x+4-9\)</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Combine like terms.
        \(y=x^2+4x-5\)</para>
      <para>Here is how to find the factored form:</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - From the standard form, find the factors of the trinomial expression \(y=x^2+4x-5\).</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Examine the trinomial expression.
        \(x^2+4x-5\)</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Factor the trinomial.
        \((x + 5)(x - 1)\)</para>
      <para>The factored form is \(y = (x + 5)(x - 1)\).</para>
      <note class="try look-at-me"/>
      <title>Try It: Equivalent Forms of Quadratics</title>
      
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          
          <para>Find the equation in standard form that is equivalent to \(y=(x-2)^2+4\).</para>
        
        <para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        
        </problem><solution>
        <para>Here is how to find the equivalent standard form:</para>
        
        <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Expand the vertex form to multiply the binomials.
          \(y=(x-2)(x-2)+4\)</para>
        
        <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Multiply the binomials.
          \(y=x^2-2x-2x+4 + 4\)</para>
        
        <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Combine like terms.
          \(y=x^2-4x+8\)</para>
      
      </solution></exercise>
      </section>
  </content>
